# ⛔ EVERYTHING UNDER testdata/ IS SIGNED BYTES, AND GIT MUST NOT TOUCH THEM.
#
# On Windows, Git checks text files out with core.autocrlf, turning every LF
# into CRLF. testdata/content is the text the fixtures sign, so on the first CI
# run every one of them failed on windows-latest only — eight detached ones at
# the message-digest check ("the content's digest is not the one the signer
# signed"), the attribute-less RSA one at the RSA check itself, and the attached
# one comparing its own correct content with the converted file. Linux and
# macOS were green throughout: the library was right and the checkout was not.
#
# `binary` means no end-of-line conversion and no textual diff, which is what a
# signed byte string needs; the .der files get it too, since a DER that happened
# to contain no NUL byte would otherwise be guessed to be text.
testdata/** binary
